E = {
100: ("When raising the hands is sunnah", [
 "Raising the hands is sunnah only at the start of each prayer; at the takbir of the qunut in witr; at the extra takbirs of the two 'Ids; on seeing the Ka'bah; on touching the Black Stone; on standing on Safa and Marwah; at the standing at 'Arafah and Muzdalifah; after stoning the first and middle jamrah; and at the tasbih after the prayers.",
 "When he has finished the two prostrations of the second rak'ah, he lays down his left foot and sits on it, keeps his right foot upright with its toes towards the qiblah, places his hands on his thighs with his fingers spread (a woman sits on her hip), and recites the tashahhud of Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه, pointing with his index finger at the testimony, raising it at the negation and lowering it at the affirmation. In the first sitting he adds nothing to the tashahhud, which is:",
 "\"All greetings, prayers and good things are for Allah. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger.\"",
 "After the first two rak'ahs he recites only al-Fatihah. Then he sits, recites the tashahhud, then sends blessings upon our master the Prophet ﷺ, then supplicates with words like the Qur'an and Sunnah, then gives salam to the right and left, saying \"Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah\", intending those with him as mentioned before.",
]),
101: ("The ruling (on leading the prayer and congregation)", ["Leading the prayer is better than giving the adhan. Praying in congregation is a sunnah (emphasised) for free men without an excuse."]),
102: ("Conditions for valid leading", ["The conditions for a man to validly lead healthy men in prayer are six: Islam, puberty, sanity, being male, being able to recite, and being free of excuses such as a nosebleed, stammering, stuttering and lisping, and of missing a condition such as purity or covering the 'awrah."]),
103: ("Conditions for validly following an imam", ["The conditions for following an imam validly are fourteen: the follower intending to follow, together with his opening takbir; a man intending to lead, which is a condition for women to validly follow him; the imam being ahead of the follower by his heel; the imam not being in a lower state than the follower; the imam not praying a different obligatory prayer from his; the imam not being a resident leading a traveller in a four-rak'ah prayer after its time has ended, nor being a late-comer (masbuq); no row of women separating the imam and follower; no river on which a boat can pass, no road on which a cart can pass, and no wall that makes it unclear when the imam moves between positions, though if it is not unclear because one can hear or see, the following is valid according to the sound view; the imam not riding while the follower is on foot, or riding a different mount; the follower not being on one boat with the imam on another not joined to it; and the follower not knowing of something in his imam that invalidates the prayer in the follower's own view, such as blood flowing or vomit after which the imam has not renewed his wudu."]),
104: ("Further rulings on following", [
 "It is valid for one with wudu to follow one with tayammum, one who washed his feet to follow one who wiped over khuffs, one standing to follow one sitting or a hunchback, one praying by gestures to follow someone like him, and one praying voluntarily to follow one praying an obligatory prayer.",
 "If it becomes clear that his imam's prayer was invalid, he repeats it.",
 "The imam must inform the people that they must repeat their prayer as far as he is able, according to the chosen view.",
]),
105: ("What excuses one from the congregation", ["Attending the congregation is excused by one of eighteen things: rain, cold, fear, darkness, imprisonment, blindness, paralysis, a cut-off hand and foot, illness, being crippled, mud, chronic disability, old age, revising fiqh with a group that he would miss, food being present that he craves, intending to travel, looking after a sick person, and a strong wind at night but not in the day. If he misses the congregation for one of these excuses that permit staying behind, he receives its reward."]),
106: ("Who has most right to lead", ["If among those present there is no owner of the house, no appointed imam and no one in authority, the most knowledgeable has most right to lead, then the best reciter, then the most scrupulous, then the eldest, then the best in character, then the best in appearance, then the noblest in lineage, then the best in voice, then the one with the cleanest clothes. If they are equal, lots are drawn, or the people choose; if they disagree, what the majority chooses counts. If they put forward someone other than the most entitled, they have done wrong."]),
107: ("Whose leading is disliked", ["It is disliked for these to lead: a slave, a blind man, a Bedouin, one born of fornication, an ignorant person, a sinner and an innovator; and it is disliked to make the prayer long, and for unclothed people or women to pray in congregation. If women do so, the imam stands in the middle of them, as with the unclothed."]),
108: ("The order of the rows", [
 "One follower stands on the imam's right, and more than one behind him.",
 "The men form rows, then the boys, then the khuntha, then the women.",
]),
109: ("What the follower does when his imam finishes a wajib act and the like", ["If the imam gives salam before the follower has finished the tashahhud, he completes it. If the imam raises his head before the follower has said the tasbih three times in ruku' or prostration, he follows him. If the imam adds a prostration, or stands up after the final sitting by mistake, the follower does not follow him; and if the imam confirms the extra rak'ah with a prostration, the follower gives salam alone. If the imam stands up before the final sitting by mistake, the follower waits for him; and if the follower gives salam before the imam confirms the extra rak'ah with a prostration, his obligatory prayer is invalid. It is disliked for the follower to give salam after the imam's tashahhud but before his salam."]),
110: ("The remembrances after the obligatory prayer", ["Standing for the sunnah straight after the obligatory prayer is sunnah. It is reported from Shams al-A'immah al-Halwani that there is no harm in reciting the wirds between the obligatory prayer and the sunnah. It is recommended for the imam, after his salam, to move to his left for voluntary prayer after the obligatory prayer, and then to face the people. They seek Allah's forgiveness three times, recite Ayat al-Kursi and the Mu'awwidhat, say subhan Allah thirty-three times, al-hamdu lillah likewise and Allahu akbar likewise, then say: \"There is no god but Allah alone, without partner; His is the dominion and His the praise, and He has power over all things.\" Then they supplicate for themselves and for the Muslims with their hands raised, and wipe their faces with them at the end."]),
111: ("Chapter: What invalidates the prayer", [
 "It is sixty-eight things:",
 "speaking, even by forgetfulness or mistake; supplicating with something like our speech; greeting with salam with the intention of greeting, even forgetfully; returning a greeting by tongue or by shaking hands; excessive movement; turning the chest away from the qiblah; eating anything from outside the mouth, even a little; eating what is between the teeth if it is the size of a chickpea; drinking; clearing the throat without an excuse; saying uff; groaning; sighing; crying out loud from pain or misfortune, but not from remembering Paradise or the Fire; replying to a sneezer with yarhamuk Allah; answering one who asks about a partner (to Allah) with la ilaha illa'llah, bad news with inna lillah, good news with al-hamdu lillah, or something amazing with la ilaha illa'llah or subhan Allah; anything intended as an answer, such as \"O Yahya, hold the Book\"; one with tayammum seeing water; the period of one wiping over khuffs ending, or his taking them off; an illiterate person learning a verse; one without clothing finding a covering; one praying by gestures becoming able to bow and prostrate; remembering a missed prayer for one who must keep the order; appointing as successor someone not fit to lead; the sun rising during Fajr, passing its zenith during the 'Id prayers, or the time of 'Asr coming in during Jumu'ah; a splint falling off after healing; the excuse of an excused person ending; hadath on purpose or caused by someone else; fainting; insanity; janabah by looking or a wet dream; standing level with a woman who is desired, in a complete prayer they share from its opening, in one place without a barrier, when he intended to lead her; one whose wudu broke uncovering his 'awrah, even if he had to, like a woman uncovering her forearm for wudu; his reciting while going to or returning from wudu; staying for the length of a pillar after his wudu broke while awake; passing a nearby water source for another; leaving the masjid thinking his wudu has broken; passing the rows, outside a masjid, thinking so; turning away thinking he has no wudu, or that his wiping period has ended, or that he has a missed prayer or impurity on him, even if he does not leave the masjid, and it is better to start again; prompting someone other than his imam; and saying a takbir intending to move to another prayer. This applies if these happen before the final sitting for the length of the tashahhud. Also invalidating: lengthening the hamzah in the takbir; reading what he has not memorised from a Mushaf; performing a pillar, or having time to, with the 'awrah uncovered or with impurity that prevents prayer; the follower going ahead of his imam in a pillar that the imam does not share in; a late-comer following the imam in the prostration of forgetfulness; not repeating the final sitting after performing a prostration of the prayer itself that he remembered after the sitting; not repeating a pillar performed while asleep; loud laughter of a late-comer's imam, or his deliberate hadath, after the final sitting; and giving salam after two rak'ahs in a prayer that is not two rak'ahs, thinking he is a traveller, or that it is Jumu'ah, or that it is tarawih when it is 'Isha', or being new to Islam and thinking the obligatory prayer is two rak'ahs.",
]),
112: ("What does not invalidate the prayer", ["If one praying looks at something written and understands it, or eats what is between his teeth when it is less than a chickpea without much movement, or someone passes in the place of his prostration, it is not invalid, though the one passing is sinful. Nor is it invalid by his looking with desire at the private part of his divorced wife, according to the chosen view, even though that confirms taking her back."]),
113: ("What is disliked for one praying", ["Seventy-seven things are disliked for one praying: leaving a wajib or sunnah on purpose; such as playing with his clothes or body; moving pebbles, except once to prostrate; cracking the fingers; interlacing them; putting the hands on the hips; turning the neck; squatting (iq'a'); laying the forearms on the ground; rolling up the sleeves; praying in trousers alone when able to wear a shirt; returning a greeting by gesture; sitting cross-legged without an excuse; tying the hair; i'tijar, which is binding the head with a cloth and leaving the middle uncovered; gathering up one's clothes; letting them hang down loosely; wrapping oneself so that one cannot bring out one's hands; putting the cloth under the right armpit and throwing both ends over the left shoulder; reciting in other than the standing position; lengthening the first rak'ah in voluntary prayer; making the second rak'ah longer than the first in any prayer; repeating a surah in one rak'ah of an obligatory prayer; reciting a surah that comes before the one he recited (in order); separating by one surah between two surahs he recites in two rak'ahs; smelling perfume; fanning oneself with one's clothing or a fan once or twice; turning the fingers or toes away from the qiblah in prostration and elsewhere; not placing the hands on the knees in ruku'; yawning; closing the eyes; raising them to the sky; stretching; a little movement; picking up a louse and killing it; covering the nose and mouth; putting something in the mouth that prevents the sunnah recitation; prostrating on the fold of one's turban or on images; prostrating on the forehead only, without an excuse for the nose; praying on a road, in a bathhouse, in a toilet, in a graveyard, on another's land without his consent, or near impurity; praying while holding back urine, faeces or wind; praying with impurity that is not liquid, unless he fears missing the time or the congregation, and otherwise he should stop; praying in work clothes; praying bareheaded, except out of humility and lowliness; praying with food present that he wants, or anything that occupies the mind and spoils humility; counting verses and tasbih with the hand; the imam standing (alone) inside the mihrab, or on a raised place, or on the ground alone; standing behind a row that has a gap; wearing clothing with images; there being an image above his head, behind him, in front of him or beside him, unless it is small, or has its head cut off, or is of something without a soul; there being in front of him an oven or brazier with embers, or people sleeping; wiping dust that does not bother him from his forehead during the prayer; fixing a surah so that he recites no other, unless for ease or for the blessing of the Prophet's ﷺ recitation; and not putting up a sutrah where he thinks people may pass in front of him."]),
114: ("Using a sutrah and stopping those who pass in front", ["If he thinks people may pass, it is recommended to set up a sutrah a cubit or more in length and as thick as a finger. The sunnah is to stand close to it and have it in line with one of his eyebrows, not directly in front of him. If he finds nothing to set up, let him draw a line lengthwise; and they said: across, like a crescent."]),
115: ("Stopping one who passes in front", ["It is recommended not to push away one who passes, but it is allowed to stop him with a gesture or with saying subhan Allah; combining the two is disliked. He may stop him by raising his voice in recitation. A woman stops him with a gesture, or by clapping the back of her right fingers on her left palm; she does not raise her voice, as that is a fitnah. One does not fight the one passing; what has come about that is interpreted as having been when such action was permitted, and it has been abrogated."]),
116: ("What is not disliked for one praying", [
 "It is not disliked for him to tie a belt around his waist, or to wear a sword or the like if he is not distracted by its movement; nor not to put his hands through the openings and slits of his garment, according to the chosen view; nor to face a Mushaf, a hanging sword, the back of someone sitting and talking, a candle or a lamp, according to the sound view; to prostrate on a rug with images if he does not prostrate on them; to kill a snake or scorpion he fears will harm him, even with several blows and turning away from the qiblah, according to the clearer view.",
 "There is no harm in shaking his garment so that it does not stick to his body in ruku', nor in wiping dust or grass from his forehead after finishing the prayer, or before finishing it if it harms him or distracts him from the prayer; nor in looking from the corners of his eyes without turning his face. There is no harm in praying on carpets, rugs and felt.",
 "It is better to pray on the ground or on what grows from it. There is no harm in repeating a surah in both rak'ahs of a voluntary prayer.",
]),
117: ("When breaking off the prayer is wajib, and when permitted", ["Breaking off the prayer is wajib when someone in distress calls on the one praying for help, but not for the call of one of his parents. It is permitted to break it off for the theft of something worth a dirham, even if it belongs to someone else; for fear of a wolf attacking sheep; for fear that a blind person will fall into a well and the like; and if a midwife fears the child will die, and otherwise there is no harm in her delaying the prayer and attending to the child. Likewise a traveller who fears thieves or highway robbers may delay the current prayer."]),
118: ("The punishment of one who leaves prayer and fasting", ["One who leaves the prayer deliberately out of laziness is beaten severely until blood flows, and imprisoned until he prays; likewise one who leaves the fast of Ramadan. He is not killed unless he denies either of them or treats them with contempt."]),
119: ("The ruling of witr and how it is prayed", ["Witr is wajib. It is three rak'ahs with one salam. In each rak'ah he recites al-Fatihah and a surah, sits after the first two and recites only the tashahhud, and does not recite the opening praise when he stands for the third. When he finishes reciting the surah in it, he raises his hands to his ears, says the takbir, and makes qunut standing before ruku', throughout the year. He does not make qunut in any prayer other than witr."]),
120: ("The meaning and words of qunut", [
 "Qunut means supplication. It is to say:",
 "\"O Allah, we seek Your help, Your guidance and Your forgiveness, we repent to You, believe in You and rely on You. We praise You with all good, we thank You and are not ungrateful to You, and we cast off and leave whoever disobeys You. O Allah, You alone we worship, to You we pray and prostrate, to You we hasten and strive. We hope for Your mercy and fear Your punishment; truly Your punishment will overtake the disbelievers. And may Allah send blessings and peace upon our master the Prophet and his family.\"",
 "The follower recites the qunut like the imam.",
]),
121: ("The supplication", [
 "When the imam begins the supplication after what has been mentioned, Abu Yusuf رحمه الله said they follow him and recite it with him; Muhammad said they do not follow him, but say amin.",
 "The supplication is this:",
 "\"O Allah, guide us by Your grace among those You have guided, grant us well-being among those You have granted well-being, take us into Your care among those You have taken into Your care, bless us in what You have given, and protect us from the evil of what You have decreed; for You decree and none can decree against You. Whoever You befriend is never humbled, and whoever You take as an enemy is never honoured. Blessed are You, our Lord, and exalted. And may Allah send blessings and peace upon our master Muhammad, his family and his Companions.\"",
]),
122: ("Further rulings of qunut", [
 "One who cannot recite the qunut well says \"O Allah, forgive me\" three times, or \"Our Lord, give us good in this world and good in the Hereafter, and protect us from the punishment of the Fire\", or \"O Lord, O Lord, O Lord\".",
 "If he follows someone who makes qunut in Fajr, he stands with him in his qunut silently, according to the clearer view, with his hands by his sides.",
 "If he forgets the qunut in witr and remembers it in ruku' or after rising from it, he does not make qunut. If he makes qunut after raising his head from ruku', he does not repeat the ruku', and he makes the prostration of forgetfulness because the qunut has left its proper place.",
 "If the imam bows before the follower has finished the qunut, or before he has begun it, and he fears missing the ruku', he follows his imam.",
 "If the imam leaves the qunut, the follower makes it if he can still join the imam in ruku'; otherwise he follows him. If he joins the imam in the ruku' of the third rak'ah of witr, he has caught the qunut, and does not make it in what he makes up.",
 "Witr is prayed in congregation only in Ramadan. Praying it in congregation in Ramadan is better than praying it alone at the end of the night, in the choice of Qadi Khan, who said this is the sound view; others declared the opposite sound.",
]),
123: ("The emphasised sunnahs", ["The emphasised sunnahs are: two rak'ahs before Fajr; two after Zuhr, after Maghrib and after 'Isha'; and four before Zuhr, and before and after Jumu'ah, with one salam."]),
124: ("The recommended prayers", ["It is recommended to pray four before 'Asr, four before and after 'Isha', and six after Maghrib."]),
125: ("Various rulings", [
 "In the first sitting of an emphasised four-rak'ah sunnah he recites only the tashahhud,",
 "and in the third rak'ah he does not recite the opening supplication, unlike the recommended prayers.",
 "If he prays a voluntary prayer of more than two rak'ahs and sits only at its end, it is valid as recommended, because it has become one prayer, and the obligatory sitting in it is the last.",
 "It is disliked to pray more than four with one salam in the day and more than eight at night. Best in both is four at a time according to Abu Hanifah; according to the two companions, best at night is two by two, and fatwa is given on that.",
 "Prayer at night is better than prayer in the day, and long standing is more beloved than many prostrations.",
]),
126: ("Greeting the masjid", [
 "It is sunnah to greet the masjid with two rak'ahs before sitting. Performing the obligatory prayer takes its place, as does any prayer performed on entering, without intending the greeting.",
 "Two rak'ahs after wudu before it dries are recommended, and four or more at Duha.",
 "The night prayer, the prayer of istikharah and the prayer of need are recommended.",
]),
127: ("Reviving the nights", ["It is recommended to revive the last ten nights of Ramadan, the nights of the two 'Ids, the nights of the ten days of Dhu'l-Hijjah and the night of the middle of Sha'ban. It is disliked to gather in the masjids to revive any of these nights."]),
128: ("Voluntary prayer sitting", ["It is permitted to pray voluntary prayer sitting while able to stand, but he has half the reward of one standing, unless he has an excuse. He sits as in the tashahhud, according to the chosen view. It is permitted to complete it sitting after beginning it standing, without dislike, according to the soundest view."]),
129: ("Voluntary prayer on a mount", ["He may pray voluntary prayers riding outside the town, by gestures, in whatever direction his mount faces. He may continue the prayer if he dismounts, but not if he mounts, even in the regular sunnahs. It is reported from Abu Hanifah رحمه الله that he dismounts for the sunnah of Fajr, because it is more emphasised than the others."]),
130: ("Leaning", [
 "It is permitted for one praying voluntarily to lean on something if he is tired, without dislike; without an excuse it is disliked, according to the clearer view, as poor manners. Impurity on the mount, even on the saddle and stirrups, does not prevent the prayer on it from being valid, according to the soundest view.",
 "The prayer of one walking is not valid, by consensus.",
]),
131: ("Obligatory and wajib prayers on a mount", ["Obligatory prayers are not valid on a mount, nor wajib prayers such as witr and vowed prayers, nor a voluntary prayer that was begun and then broken, nor the funeral prayer, nor a prostration for a verse recited on the ground; except out of necessity, such as fearing for himself, his mount or his clothes from a thief if he dismounts, fearing a predator, mud on the ground, the mount being unruly, or finding no one to help him back on because he is unable."]),
132: ("Praying in a litter", ["Praying in a litter on a mount is like praying on the mount, whether it is moving or standing still. If a piece of wood is put under the litter so that it rests on the ground, it counts as the ground, and an obligatory prayer is valid in it standing."]),
133: ("Praying on a ship", [
 "Praying an obligatory prayer on a moving ship sitting, without an excuse, is valid according to Abu Hanifah, with ruku' and prostration. The two companions said it is valid only with an excuse, and this is the clearer view. An excuse is like dizziness or being unable to get off.",
 "It is not permitted on it by gestures, by agreement. A ship anchored in open sea and moved strongly by the wind is like a moving ship; otherwise it is like one standing still, according to the soundest view. If it is tied to the shore, praying in it sitting is not permitted, by consensus. If he prays standing and part of the ship rests on the ground, the prayer is valid; otherwise it is not, according to the chosen view, unless he cannot get off.",
]),
134: ("The qiblah on a ship", ["One praying on it faces the qiblah at the start of the prayer, and whenever the ship turns away from it he turns towards it during the prayer, so that he completes it facing the qiblah."]),
135: ("The ruling of tarawih", [
 "Tarawih is sunnah for men and women, and praying it in congregation is a communal sunnah.",
 "Its time is after the 'Isha' prayer. It is valid to pray witr before or after tarawih. It is recommended to delay tarawih to a third or half of the night, and it is not disliked to delay it beyond that, according to the sound view.",
]),
136: ("Its number and how it is prayed", [
 "It is twenty rak'ahs with ten salams. It is recommended to sit after every four for as long as they took, and likewise between the fifth tarwihah and witr.",
 "It is sunnah to complete the Qur'an in it once in the month, according to the sound view.",
 "If the people tire of it, he recites as much as will not drive them away, according to the chosen view. He does not leave the blessings on our master the Prophet ﷺ in any tashahhud of it, even if the people tire, according to the chosen view, nor the opening praise or the tasbih of ruku' and prostration; but he leaves the (final) supplication if the people tire. Tarawih is not made up if missed, alone or in congregation.",
]),
137: ("Chapter: Praying inside the Ka'bah", [
 "Obligatory and voluntary prayers are valid inside it, and likewise on top of it even without a sutrah, but that is disliked, as poor manners in standing above it.",
 "Whoever inside it or on top of it turns his back to anything other than the face of his imam, his prayer is valid; if he turns his back to his imam's face, it is not valid.",
 "It is valid to follow an imam inside it from outside, with the door open.",
 "If they form a circle around it with the imam outside, it is valid, except for one who is closer to it than the imam on the imam's side.",
]),
138: ("Chapter: The prayer of the traveller", [
 "The journey in the Shari'ah",
 "The least journey that changes the rulings is a journey of three days of the shortest days of the year, at a moderate pace with rests.",
 "The moderate pace is that of camels and walking on foot on land, what suits the mountains in the mountains, and a moderate wind at sea.",
]),
139: ("Shortening the prayer", ["One who intends to travel shortens the four-rak'ah obligatory prayer, even if his journey is sinful, once he has passed the houses of where he lives and what is connected to them of its outskirts. If the outskirts are separated by a farm or the distance of a bowshot, he need not pass them."]),
140: ("The outskirts (fina')", ["The outskirts are the place set aside for the town's needs, such as racing horses and burying the dead."]),
141: ("Conditions of travel", [
 "Three things are required for the intention of travel to be valid: being independent in one's decisions, puberty, and the journey not being less than three days.",
 "So one who has not passed the buildings of his home does not shorten; nor one who has passed them but is a child, or a dependent whose leader has not intended travel, like a wife with her husband, a slave with his master, or a soldier with his commander; nor one intending less than three days. The intention of residence or travel is that of the leader, not the dependent, if the dependent knows the leader's intention, according to the soundest view.",
]),
142: ("The ruling of shortening", ["Shortening is obligatory ('azimah) for us. If he completes four rak'ahs and sits after the first two, his prayer is valid with dislike; otherwise it is not valid, unless he intended residence when he stood up for the third."]),
143: ("How long one shortens, and intending residence", [
 "He keeps shortening until he enters his town, or intends to stay half a month in a town or village. He shortens if he intends less than that, or does not intend anything and stays for years.",
 "An intention of residence is not valid in two towns if he has not fixed where he will spend the night; nor in the desert, except for tent-dwellers; nor for our army in enemy territory, nor in our own land while besieging rebels.",
]),
144: ("A traveller following a resident and the reverse", [
 "If a traveller follows a resident within the time, it is valid and he completes four. After the time it is not valid. The reverse is valid in both cases, and it is recommended for the imam to say: \"Complete your prayer, for I am a traveller\"; he should say it before beginning the prayer.",
 "The resident does not recite in what he completes after his travelling imam finishes, according to the soundest view.",
]),
145: ("Making up missed prayers", ["A prayer missed while travelling is made up as two rak'ahs, and one missed while resident as four; what counts is the end of the prayer time."]),
}
